 #uuid "f32746fa-b21d-5419-89a6-e1ebe75e57bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263794} {:answer/id #uuid "8f249884-b04c-52e4-af65-c55c41e6fbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263795} {:answer/id #uuid "ba39b93f-7e33-570a-ba81-c570bc0f4f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263796} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5a0010-dc85-5817-8487-04dd367e4f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f32746fa-b21d-5419-89a6-e1ebe75e57bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263794} :answer-field/id #uuid "c634d0fb-2446-54d9-8a57-37482f87fb6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263792}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afe39bd4-57b4-5ba4-a50a-d82befb4830a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139689" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948864/assets/6c52f315d079b82fb729c4ab47f210ec4bf2683d0dfe1adfe79c94db9fbc5f63.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $a$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $b$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $c$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $B,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** |\n| From: | **A** | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+0=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3cb5e3f-6eb7-5cd1-99a9-09a46a5646f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263800} {:answer/id #uuid "308fb8b3-4ac9-5236-b39c-91e4c02563d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263801} {:answer/id #uuid "5330ce6c-114b-5877-8a3e-880f0010dcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263802} {:answer/id #uuid "41608fd3-102c-5952-93ca-4fdea0500635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263803} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e3b76c-622a-5f89-bec8-aafd6d5a3adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3cb5e3f-6eb7-5cd1-99a9-09a46a5646f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263800} :answer-field/id #uuid "53948a08-104a-57d1-8b95-c984d6d88e5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263799}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c07159f7-26f5-5c5c-bce2-2dba546db581" :question/math-academy-id "q-139719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948864/assets/0ed581569bdc7c4f269f4cdf48d3ae20205627312641ff5b06daa2dca5a0e168.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows bus routes between four stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the four stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** | **D** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $a$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $0$ | $c$ | $1$ |  |\n| **D** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There are $2$ arrows starting at Station A and ending at Station D. So, there are two routes from $A$ to $D,$ and so $a=2.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station B and ending at Station C. So, there is only one route from $B$ to $C,$ and so $b=1.$\n\n- There are no arrows starting at Station C and ending at Station C. So, there are no routes from $C$ to $C,$ and so $c=0.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n|  |  | To: |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **A** | **B** | **C** | **D** |\n| From: | **A** | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| **B** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n| **C** | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |\n| **D** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aead4910-2fd6-52bd-a959-abf2ebb06c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263807} {:answer/id #uuid "ac16c932-820d-5da1-8e80-abff7a69bc7f" :answer/ty